 #edb/ref 17592186277609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278077 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278077 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278077 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186277609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278077 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c8880f9f-c13c-59f9-8dce-1a13792353a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278573 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278573 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278573 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278573 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n- $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n- $\\text{int}\\,A=A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278573 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35b15061-fc8b-55f3-8742-4ca105208a91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278573 #edb/ref 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/84/8414de2e05da43c03fae3c8b1ff2102401e990188e9fd4ee59a24308ec34b086.png)\n\n \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains some (not all) of its boundary points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. As we have seen, $∂A$ consists of two concentric circles: the outer one is in $A,$ while the inner one is not.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ contains some of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\ne A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278580 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278580 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161643" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278580 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278580 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4y>{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅$\n- $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n- $∂A⊂A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278580 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "32d0249e-396e-5abf-9c3e-a9a01870f500" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278580 #edb/ref 1074 "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a3/a380742f47d97062d56b482592cd2d2961dcc40262131a99886b815c507ab6e3.png)\n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/26/26d264d702368ed6fe05577d7c34630bd16b41bdba8d206dd4db39763db02938.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A\\cap ∂A=∅,$ where $∅$ denotes the empty set.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. In this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points, so we must have $∂A⊈A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and  II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278638 #edb/ref 1080 "III  only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278638 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278638 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "052b75ab-358d-5073-b438-8aa